WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.270 --> 00:00:05.340
As any cat owner can tell you, these fuzzy furballs spend

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:07.710
a lot of time grooming themselves.

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:08.960
According to some studies,

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:11.459
up to a quarter of their waking hours.

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:14.260
Cat’s sandpapery tongues are covered in

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:17.580
sharp, little cones known as papillae.

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:19.960
Researchers wanted a closer look at how these papillae

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:22.660
actually help cats groom both their

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:25.480
outer and inner layers of fur.

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:28.980
So they created 3D scans of tongues from six species

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:32.571
of cats: the domestic cat, the bobcat,

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:36.860
the Cougar, snow leopard, tiger and lion.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:39.180
Zooming in revealed that the papillae have

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:42.260
a U-shaped cavity at their tips, which has

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:46.300
a wicking effect and helps carry droplets of saliva

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:49.120
through that top layer of fur down to the cat's skin.

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:52.820
The scientists also filmed several cats grooming.

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:55.300
When they slowed down the footage, they noticed that

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:57.560
the cats flared their tongues so

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:00.080
the papillae were perpendicular to the surface

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:02.920
of the tongue, which maximizes how much fur

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:04.930
they came into contact with.

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:07.020
The researchers also used a thermal camera

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:09.469
and found that depositing saliva on their

00:01:09.469 --> 00:01:13.460
skin helps the cats cool off if they get overheated.

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:17.040
The researchers also designed a hairbrush,

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.840
based on the shape and configuration of the papillae

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:21.040
on a cat’s tongue.

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:22.400
Compared to a regular hairbrush,

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:26.020
the brush tugged less and was easier to clean.

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:28.540
Beyond helping humans get tangles out of their hair

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:30.340
or cat fur out of their carpets,

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:33.820
the scientists say the brush could potentially help deliver

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:37.280
medicine or cleaning solution directly to cats’ skin,

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:40.350
which could help improve treatments for cat allergies.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.340
For now, it’s a closer look at the surprisingly complex

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:48.100
every day routines of our fuzzy feline friends.

